import { paceSelect } from "./preferences.js";

export function showRules({
  game,
  speed,
  setSpeed,
  showModal,
  $,
  startTutorial,
}) {
  showModal(
    "The Art of Playing Together",
    `${startTutorial ? '<p><button type="button" id="rules-tutorial" class="gold-btn">Try the Interactive Tutorial</button></p>' : ""}<p>The player opposite you is your partner. Play runs counterclockwise: you → right → partner → left. In AI Practice, Jade is your partner. Online 2v2 has two fixed teams; the host can fill open seats with AI players.</p><h3>The Turn</h3><p>Select cards, then choose <b>Play Cards</b>. Beat the current combination with the same type, play a bomb, or pass. Three consecutive passes clear the table. The last player leads again; if they have finished, their partner leads.</p><h3>Seven Ordinary Combinations</h3><table><tr><td>Single / Pair / Triple</td><td>1 / 2 / 3 cards of one rank</td></tr><tr><td>Full House</td><td>A triple + a different-rank pair</td></tr><tr><td>Straight</td><td>5 consecutive ranks</td></tr><tr><td>Tube</td><td>3 consecutive pairs</td></tr><tr><td>Plate</td><td>2 consecutive triples</td></tr></table><p>Singles, pairs, triples, full houses, and equal-sized rank bombs use level order: Big Joker &gt; Small Joker &gt; Level Rank &gt; A … 2. Only two Small Jokers or two Big Jokers form a pair. A full house is ranked by its triple.</p><p>Runs always use natural order. A can be low or high, never wrap: A2345 and 10JQKA are valid; QKA23 is not. Lowest tube: AA2233. Lowest plate: AAA222.</p><h3>Wild Hearts</h3><p>Both heart cards of the Level Rank are wild. They can represent any card except a joker. A single wild or a pair of wilds stays at the Level Rank. When multiple declarations are possible, use the combination menu before playing. The declared substitutions appear on the table.</p><h3>Bombs, from Low to High</h3><p>4 of a kind → 5 of a kind → 5-card straight flush → 6 → 7 → 8 → 9 → 10 of a kind → all four jokers. Higher bombs beat lower bombs. Within one size, compare ranks; straight flushes compare top cards, regardless of suit.</p><h3>The Climb to Ace</h3><p>Only the winning team advances and becomes the Level Team. Finishing <b>1st & 2nd: +${game.doubleUp}</b>; <b>1st & 3rd: +2</b>; <b>1st & 4th: +1</b>. Promotions stop at Ace. Win as the Level Team on Ace with 1–2 or 1–3 to win the match.</p><p>A 1–4 result at Ace is a failed attempt. Three failed Ace attempts, even across separate visits to Ace, reset that team to 2. Losing at Ace when the opponent’s final play is entirely declared aces also resets the Level Team to 2. Challengers climb from their own level as usual and must become the Level Team before they can win the match.</p><h3>Tribute</h3><p>After a 1–2 finish, both losers pay their highest non-wild cards. First place receives the higher one. Otherwise, only last place pays first place. Winners return a card of 10 or lower (any card if they hold none). Each person chooses their own return; AI players choose automatically.</p><p>Both Big Jokers among the two paying losers cancel double tribute. For single tribute, the payer must hold both. The higher or sole tribute payer leads. Without tribute, the previous winner leads.</p><h3>Table Conventions</h3><p>Equal tribute cards are ordered counterclockwise from the previous winner; the first payer leads. The first hand’s opening player is random, equivalent to drawing the turned-up card. Shuffling and drawing are automatic. A hand ends as soon as one partnership is out.</p><h3>AI Pace</h3>${paceSelect("speed-setting", speed)}<p><small>AI players favor longer combinations, protect their partner’s lead, and conserve bombs. Every level sees only its own cards, the card counts, and the cards already played.</small></p>`,
  );
  if (startTutorial) $("rules-tutorial").onclick = startTutorial;
  $("speed-setting").onchange = (e) => setSpeed(Number(e.target.value));
}
